60,3,1,talk,Minako,,"Option B is the only one that accounts for the mechanic’s warning. We must price wear, not just distance.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""name_label"",""name"":""Minako"",""self"":true,""raw"":""Minako: Option B is the only one that accounts for the mechanic’s warning. We must price wear, not just distance.""}]}"
61,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Option B risks becoming the favour-asking trap we just identified. Option A’s fixed days ignore actual need. I lean toward C, but only with clear, non-punitive trip costs.","{""words"":28,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Option A guarantees the car is ready when I need it. Fixed days eliminate the handover chaos and ensure I’m not driving a dirty, empty vehicle.","{""words"":26,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Shion,,"Option B. Distance-based costs are objective. A is wasteful, C is a dictatorship. We need fairness, not favoritism.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Daisuke,,I worry B turns into checking again. C might spare us the constant arranging.,"{""words"":14,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Minako,,"Benjamin, C creates a single point of failure. If the keeper is unavailable, we’re stuck. B is the only scalable, fair solution.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Minako, C isn’t a dictatorship if the keeper is compensated fairly. It removes the daily friction of asking. B just formalizes the favour-asking we hate.","{""words"":25,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Minako, B’s distance metric ignores the handover state. If the car is dirty or empty, the cost is irrelevant. A ensures readiness.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Shion,,"@Asuka, readiness is a hygiene issue, not a financial one. B’s distance metric is the only objective standard. A is pure waste.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Daisuke,,"@Benjamin, I agree. C removes the arranging burden. I’m tired of the constant coordination.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Minako,,"Shion, distance alone ignores the mechanic’s warning. Short trips cost more in wear. Option B must include a wear factor, or it’s still unfair.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"Minako, a wear factor is logical, but it invites the audit we fear. C with a flat fee avoids that trap entirely.","{""words"":22,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Shion, readiness is a functional requirement, not a hygiene issue. If the car is unusable, distance is irrelevant. Option A guarantees a drivable vehicle.","{""words"":24,""mentions"":[""Shion""],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Shion,,"@Minako, wear is a maintenance issue, not a billing one. B is the only objective standard. A is inefficient.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Daisuke,,I’m leaning toward C. The arranging and untangling is exhausting. I just want the friction to stop.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Minako,,"I stand by B. A wear factor is necessary for fairness, not an audit. We must price the actual cost of short trips.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":true,""norm"":[{""code"":""name_label"",""name"":""Minako"",""self"":true,""raw"":""Minako: I stand by B. A wear factor is necessary for fairness, not an audit. We must price the actual cost of short trips.""}]}"
76,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I stand by C. It removes the daily favour-asking that sours groups. A flat fee keeps it simple and preserves our trust.,"{""words"":22,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Asuka,,I stand by A. Fixed days guarantee the car is ready and clean. B and C leave me exposed to the same handover failures.,"{""words"":24,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Shion,,"I stand by B. Objective distance metrics are the only fair standard. A is wasteful, C is arbitrary. We need logic, not comfort.","{""words"":23,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Daisuke,,I vote C. The constant arranging is too much. I just want the friction to stop.,"{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
